Ladybird resolves filter regions and primitive subregions as Chrome 157
and Firefox 151 do, verified at runtime on 24 cases: default, fractional
and percentage subregions in both units, zero widths, feImage fitting,
and the filter region on SVG and HTML elements. The two differ on a
negative width: Chrome disables the primitive, as the spec says, and
Ladybird does too, while Firefox ignores the attribute. WebKit
(Playwright’s 26.5 build) treats a zero-size primitive as an error that
hides the element, so it’s no reference here.

For an HTML element, Blink’s PaintLayer::UpdateFilterReferenceBox()
takes LocalBoundingBoxIncludingSelfPaintingDescendants(), the box’s
visual overflow grown by its self-painting descendants, and its
ComputeFilterViewport() takes the box’s size for userSpaceOnUse
percentages. Ladybird has no visual overflow, so it grows the border box
by the scrollable overflow and the descendants’ border boxes instead.
All three engines render filter-region-transformed-child-001 and
filter-region-negative-positioned-child-001 that way.

A primitive’s default subregion is the union of its inputs’ subregions
as defined, before the filter region clips them: The spec says “the
subregions defined for all referenced nodes”. Chrome 157 does that, in
user space and in bounding-box units: Blink’s SetStandardAttributes()
stores the unclipped subregion that DefaultFilterPrimitiveSubregion()
then unions. Firefox 151 unions the clipped ones instead, since Gecko’s
SVGFilterInstance stores each PrimitiveSubregion() intersected with
FilterSpaceBounds(). WebKit 26.5 never looks at the inputs: Its
FilterEffectGeometry::primitiveSubregion() defaults a missing attribute
to the filter region instead.

An feImage whose image has an aspect ratio but no width or height (an
SVG with only a viewBox) fits its subregion at that ratio, as in Chrome
157 and Firefox 151, verified with meet, slice, none, and xMinYMin, and
with an SVG that has a width but no height. Blink draws an SVG image at
its container’s size, so the image’s own viewBox does the fitting.
WebKit 26.5 draws nothing there.

A descendant that a transform moves out past the box’s start edge (a
child with translateX(-100px), e.g.) counts toward the object bounding
box too: Ladybird maps each descendant’s border box through its own
transform and its ancestors’ up to the filtered box. Chrome 157, Firefox
151 and WebKit 26.5 all draw such a child whole.
